(window.LADDER_CARDS["26a"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["26a"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"26a1.1","q":"When does a sum Σ zₖ of complex numbers settle, and what does the plane-size technique give?","a":"when its partial sums head to one complex number; and if the sizes Σ |zₖ| settle, then Σ zₖ settles","w":["when its terms head to 0; and if Σ zₖ settles, then Σ |zₖ| settles","when its floor parts settle; the upright parts then follow by the plane-size technique","when Σ |zₖ| settles and only then; a sum that does not settle in size never settles"],"why":"Settling in size is enough, though it is not needed."},
 {"u":"26a2.1","q":"What is e^z for a complex z, why does its sum settle, and what did the new definition change on the floor?","a":"e^z = Σ zᵏ/k!; the sizes sum to Σ |z|ᵏ/k! = e^|z|, which settles, so the plane-size technique applies; on the floor nothing changed, it is the old e^x","w":["e^z = Σ zᵏ/k; it settles for |z| < 1 only; on the floor it is the old e^x","e^z = Σ zᵏ/k!; it settles because its terms head to 0; on the floor it is a new function that agrees with e^x at whole x","e^z is e multiplied by itself z times; it settles by the comparison of areas; on the floor nothing changed"],"why":"The same sum, read with complex inputs."},
 {"u":"26a3.1","q":"What does the turn-growth technique say, and which pieces of the sum for e^(iθ) give each part?","a":"e^(iθ) = cos θ + i sin θ for every real θ; the even powers of iθ are real and sum to cos θ, the odd powers carry one i and sum to i sin θ","w":["e^(iθ) = cos θ + i sin θ; the odd powers sum to cos θ and the even powers to i sin θ","e^(iθ) = sin θ + i cos θ; the even powers give sin θ and the odd powers give i cos θ","e^(iθ) = e^θ(cos θ + i sin θ); the even powers give the growth and the odd powers the turn"],"why":"i² = −1 turns the even powers into the alternating sum of cos."},
 {"u":"26a4.1","q":"What is wrong with “e^(iθ) grows as θ grows”, and with “Σ zₖ settles, so Σ |zₖ| settles”?","a":"|e^(iθ)| = 1 for every θ, so it turns round the unit circle and never grows; and the plane-size technique runs one way only, since 1 − 1/2 + 1/3 − ⋯ settles while its sizes do not","w":["e^(iθ) shrinks as θ grows; and the second is right, being the plane-size technique","e^(iθ) grows only for θ > 0; and the second fails only for sums with upright parts","|e^(iθ)| = θ, so it grows slowly; and the second is the plane-size technique read backwards, which also holds"],"why":"A growth function with an upright rate turns."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["26b"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["26b"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"26b1.1","q":"What does the plane-adding technique say, and how does its proof go?","a":"e^(z + w) = e^z e^w for all z and w in ℂ; multiply the two sums, gather the terms of the same total power, and the two-term power makes each gathering (z + w)ⁿ/n!","w":["e^(z + w) = e^z + e^w; add the two sums term by term","e^(zw) = e^z e^w; multiply the two sums and gather the terms with the same product","e^(z + w) = e^z e^w for real z and w only; it is carried over from rung 17 with no new proof"],"why":"Settling in size lets the product of sums be regrouped."},
 {"u":"26b2.1","q":"What does the growth-and-turn technique say, and read as a move, what does multiplying by e^z do?","a":"for z = a + bi, e^z = e^a(cos b + i sin b), so |e^z| = e^a and e^z is never 0; multiplying by e^z stretches by e^a and turns by b","w":["e^z = e^a + i e^b, so |e^z| = e^a + e^b; multiplying by e^z stretches by e^a and slides by b","e^z = e^b(cos a + i sin a), so |e^z| = e^b; multiplying by e^z stretches by e^b and turns by a","e^z = e^a(cos b + i sin b), so e^z = 0 when cos b = 0; multiplying by e^z turns by a and stretches by b"],"why":"The floor part grows; the upright part turns."},
 {"u":"26b3.1","q":"What does the whole-turn technique say, and how does exp on the plane differ from exp on the line?","a":"e^z = 1 exactly when z = 2πik for a whole k, so e^z = e^w exactly when z − w is a whole number of 2πi; on the line exp never repeats a value, in the plane it repeats every 2πi up the upright","w":["e^z = 1 exactly when z = 0; exp never repeats a value on the line or in the plane","e^z = 1 exactly when z = πik; in the plane exp repeats every πi","e^z = 1 exactly when z = 2πk on the floor; exp repeats along the floor and not along the upright"],"why":"A whole turn brings every point back."},
 {"u":"26b4.1","q":"What is wrong with “e^z = e^w, so z = w”, and with “|e^(a + bi)| = e^(|a + bi|)”?","a":"equal values give only that z − w is a whole number of 2πi; and the size of e^(a + bi) is e^a, from the floor part alone, since the upright part only turns","w":["the first is right in the plane; the second should read |e^(a + bi)| = e^b","equal values give z = −w as well; and the size is e^a · e^b","equal values give z − w = 2πk on the floor; and the size is e^(a² + b²)"],"why":"e^(2πi) = 1 though 2πi ≠ 0, and |e^(i)| = 1, not e."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["26c"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["26c"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"26c1.1","q":"What is a logarithm of z, and what does the plane-log technique say?","a":"any w with e^w = z; 0 has none, and every z ≠ 0, written r e^(iθ), has exactly the logarithms ln r + i(θ + 2πk), one for each whole k","w":["the one w with e^w = z; every z, 0 included, has exactly one","any w with e^w = z; every z ≠ 0 has exactly two, ln r ± iθ","any w with w e^w = z; every z ≠ 0 has ln r + iθ + 2πk, one for each whole k"],"why":"The whole-turn technique gives all of them from one."},
 {"u":"26c2.1","q":"What is Log z, and what does the main-log technique say about Log(zw)?","a":"the logarithm ln r + iθ with θ in (−π, π]; e^(Log z) = z and Log agrees with ln on the positive floor, but Log(zw) = Log z + Log w only when the two turns add to an angle still in (−π, π]","w":["the logarithm with θ in [0, 2π); Log(zw) = Log z + Log w for all z and w not 0","the logarithm ln r, with the turn dropped; Log(zw) = Log z + Log w always","the logarithm with θ in (−π, π]; Log(zw) = Log z · Log w when the turns add inside (−π, π]"],"why":"When the turns overshoot, the main log is off by 2πi."},
 {"u":"26c3.1","q":"What does z^w name, and what does the many-powers technique say?","a":"e^(w log z), one value for each logarithm of z; zⁿ has one value for whole n, z^(1/n) has exactly n, and z^w has endlessly many when w is not a fraction","w":["e^(w Log z) only, so every power has one value","e^(z log w); zⁿ has n values and z^(1/n) has one","e^(w log z); every power has endlessly many values, whole powers included"],"why":"The 2πik in the logarithm is multiplied by w before exp wraps it."},
 {"u":"26c4.1","q":"What is wrong with “1 = √((−1)(−1)) = √(−1)√(−1) = −1”, and with “log 0 = −∞”?","a":"√(zw) = √z √w can fail for the main values, as Log(zw) = Log z + Log w fails when the turns overshoot; and e^w is never 0, so 0 has no logarithm, and −∞ is not a number","w":["√(−1) has no value, so the chain never starts; and log 0 = −∞ is right in the plane","the first step, 1 = √1, is the false one; and log 0 = 0","√(zw) = √z √w always holds, so 1 = −1 shows ℂ has a clash; and log 0 is 2πik"],"why":"Each root and log has several values; a technique for one choice need not hold."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["26d"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["26d"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"26d1.1","q":"When does f have a complex slope at z₀, and what does the plane-slope technique say?","a":"when (f(z₀ + h) − f(z₀))/h heads to one number as the complex h heads to 0 from every direction; sums, products, quotients and chains keep complex slopes by rung 13’s techniques, so (zⁿ)′ = nzⁿ⁻¹ and (1/z)′ = −1/z² for z ≠ 0","w":["when the rise over run settles for real h; then rung 13’s techniques carry over unchanged","when its floor part and its upright part each have a slope; then every continuous function has a complex slope","when (f(z₀ + h) − f(z₀))/|h| settles; products and chains then lose their slopes"],"why":"h may come in from any side of the plane."},
 {"u":"26d2.1","q":"What does the plane-exp-slope technique say, and which technique opens its proof?","a":"exp has a complex slope at every z and exp′(z) = exp z; the plane-adding technique opens it, writing (e^(z + h) − e^z)/h as e^z (e^h − 1)/h","w":["exp′(z) = z e^(z − 1); the plane-slope power procedure opens it","exp′(z) = exp z on the floor only; the turn-growth technique opens it","exp′(z) = i exp z; the two-ways technique opens it"],"why":"(e^h − 1)/h heads to 1 from the sum itself."},
 {"u":"26d3.1","q":"What does the two-ways technique say, and why does the flip fail it?","a":"with a complex slope at z₀, the rise over run along the floor (h = t) and along the upright (h = it) head to the same number; for the flip they head to 1 and to −1, so the flip has no complex slope anywhere","w":["the two head to numbers that are flips of each other; the flip fails because its two are equal","the two head to the same number; the flip fails because it is not continuous","along the upright the rise over run heads to i times the other; the flip passes with slope 1"],"why":"One slope must serve every direction."},
 {"u":"26d4.1","q":"What is wrong with “the rise over run settles along the floor, so f′(z₀) exists”, and with “(e^z)′ = ze^(z − 1)”?","a":"the floor is one direction of many, and the flip settles along it with no complex slope; and nzⁿ⁻¹ is for a fixed power of a moving z, while exp′ = exp","w":["the first is right once the upright agrees in size; and (e^z)′ = e^(z − 1)","the first is right for continuous f; and (e^z)′ = z e^z","the floor must be replaced by the upright; and (e^z)′ = e^z holds on the floor only"],"why":"The power technique and the exp technique answer different questions."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["26e"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["26e"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"26e1.1","q":"What is ∫_γ f(z) dz along a path γ, and what does the path-fence technique say?","a":"∫ from u to v of f(γ(t)) γ′(t) dt; and if |f| ≤ M on the path and the path has length L, then |∫_γ f(z) dz| ≤ ML","w":["∫ from u to v of f(γ(t)) dt; and |∫_γ f dz| ≤ M","∫ from u to v of f(γ(t)) γ′(t) dt; and |∫_γ f dz| ≤ M times the distance between the ends","∫ from u to v of |f(γ(t))| dt; and |∫_γ f dz| = ML"],"why":"Each small step of the path is a complex number γ′(t) dt."},
 {"u":"26e2.1","q":"What does the path-ends technique say, and which power does it leave out, and why?","a":"if F′ = f on an open set holding the path, the total is F(end) − F(start), so every loop there totals 0; it leaves out z⁻¹, whose would-be undoing z⁰/0 has no meaning, and 1/z has no undoing round 0","w":["the total is F(end) − F(start) for every continuous f; it leaves out z⁰","the total is F(start) − F(end); it leaves out z⁻², which has no slope at 0","every loop totals 2πi; it leaves out z⁻¹ because 1/z is not continuous on the circle"],"why":"zⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1) undoes zⁿ for every whole n but −1."},
 {"u":"26e3.1","q":"What does the circle-powers technique say, and why does the answer not depend on r?","a":"once round |z| = r anticlockwise, ∫ zⁿ dz is 2πi for n = −1 and 0 for every other whole n; with z = re^(it), zⁿ dz = i rⁿ⁺¹ e^(i(n + 1)t) dt, so at n = −1 the r cancels, and otherwise whole turns total 0","w":["it is 2πi rⁿ⁺¹ for every n; r stays in the answer","it is 2πi for n = 1 and 0 otherwise; r cancels because the circle is round","it is 2π for n = −1 and 0 otherwise; r cancels because dz = dt"],"why":"1/z shrinks exactly as the circle grows."},
 {"u":"26e4.1","q":"What is wrong with “∮ dz/z round |z| = 1 is [Log z] = 0”, and with “|∫_γ f dz| ≤ max|f| · |end − start|”?","a":"Log jumps by 2πi across the negative floor, so it is no undoing on the whole circle, and the total is 2πi; and the fence uses the length of the path, which for a loop is not 0 though its ends meet","w":["Log has no slope anywhere, so the total cannot be found; and the fence is right as written","the total is 0 but for a different reason; and the fence needs min|f|","Log z must be replaced by ln |z|, giving 0; and the fence uses the square of the length"],"why":"A loop has ends zero apart and length 2π."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["26f"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["26f"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"26f1.1","q":"What does the triangle-total technique say, and what is the plan of its proof?","a":"if f has a complex slope on an open set holding a closed triangle T and its inside, ∮ round T of f is 0; quarter the triangle again and again, keeping a quarter with at least a quarter of the total, close in on one point, and there the slope and the path fence squeeze the total to 0","w":["∮ round T is 2πi; walk the three sides and add","∮ round T is 0 for every continuous f; each quarter triangle carries exactly a quarter of the total","∮ round T is 0; find an undoing F on T first and use the path-ends technique"],"why":"The proof needs no undoing; it builds toward one."},
 {"u":"26f2.1","q":"What does the disc-undoing technique say, and how is the undoing F built?","a":"if f is continuous on an open disc D with a complex slope at every point but at most one, f has an undoing on D and every loop in D totals 0; F(z) is the integral of f along the straight segment from the centre c to z, and the triangle c, z, z + h gives F′ = f","w":["f has an undoing on any open set; F(z) is the integral of f round the circle through z","f has an undoing on D; F(z) = f(z)(z − c)","f has an undoing on D when it has no bad point at all; F is found from the power sum of f"],"why":"A disc holds every segment from its centre."},
 {"u":"26f3.1","q":"What does the hole technique say, and why does the disc-undoing technique not reach 1/z round 0?","a":"round |z| = 1, 1/z totals 2πi, so it has no undoing on any set holding that circle; the one excepted point must keep f continuous, and 1/z has no value at 0, so the disc has a hole","w":["1/z totals 0 round the circle; the disc-undoing technique reaches it since only one point fails","1/z totals 2πi; the disc-undoing technique fails because 1/z has no slope on the circle itself","1/z has no undoing anywhere in ℂ; no disc can hold a logarithm"],"why":"On a disc without 0, a continuous logarithm undoes 1/z."},
 {"u":"26f4.1","q":"What is wrong with “∮ dz/z round |z| = 1 is 0 by the disc-undoing technique”, and with “F(z) = ∫ over [c, z] of f works on any set”?","a":"1/z has no value at 0, and the exception point must keep f continuous; and the segment from c to z, with the triangles of the proof, must stay inside the set: a disc, not a ring","w":["the circle is not a triangle; and F works on any open set","1/z has two bad points inside; and F needs f to be a polynomial","the total is 0 but by the path-ends technique; and F works on a ring but not a disc"],"why":"The total is 2πi (26e3)."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["26g"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["26g"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"26g1.1","q":"What does the rim technique say, and why does only the k = 0 piece of the geometric sum survive the loop?","a":"for z inside the circle C, f(z) = (1/2πi) ∮_C f(ζ)/(ζ − z) dζ: the rim fixes the inside; 1/(ζ − z) opens as Σ (z − w)ᵏ/(ζ − w)ᵏ⁺¹, and by the circle-powers technique only 1/(ζ − w) totals 2πi","w":["f(z) = ∮_C f(ζ) dζ; every piece of the geometric sum totals 2πi","f(z) = (1/2πi) ∮_C f(ζ)/(ζ − z) dζ for z on C; the k = 1 piece survives","f(z) = (1/2π) ∮_C f(ζ)(ζ − z) dζ; no piece survives, which is why the total is f(z)"],"why":"Every other power of ζ − w totals 0 round the loop."},
 {"u":"26g2.1","q":"What does the rebuilt-in-the-plane technique say, and why can a function like e^(−1/x²), smooth and not its sum, not happen in the plane?","a":"a complex slope on |z − w| < R makes f = Σ uₖ(z − w)ᵏ on that whole disc, with |uₖ| ≤ M/rᵏ; one complex slope forces the power sum, and e^(−1/z²) has no complex slope at 0, since it blows up along the upright","w":["f equals its power sum only where rung 23’s remainder heads to 0; e^(−1/z²) is its own sum in the plane","f = Σ uₖ(z − w)ᵏ with |uₖ| ≤ M·rᵏ; e^(−1/z²) fails because it is not real","f equals its power sum at w only; e^(−1/z²) has a complex slope at 0 and sum 0"],"why":"In the plane, one slope gives every slope and the sum."},
 {"u":"26g3.1","q":"What does the bounded-constant technique say, how does the fence |uₖ| ≤ M/rᵏ prove it, and what does it give about polynomials?","a":"a function with a complex slope on all of ℂ and |f| ≤ M everywhere is constant; r may be as large as you like, so uₖ = 0 for every k ≥ 1; and a polynomial with no root would make 1/p such a function, so every polynomial of degree at least 1 has a root in ℂ","w":["such a function is a polynomial; the fence gives uₖ = 0 for k ≥ 2; and every polynomial is bounded","such a function is constant; the fence is used with r heading to 0; and every polynomial has a real root","such a function is 0; the fence gives u₀ = 0; and no polynomial has a root"],"why":"sin is bounded on the line, never on the plane."},
 {"u":"26g4.1","q":"What is wrong with “f(z) = (1/2πi)∮_C f(ζ)/(ζ − z) dζ for z outside C”?","a":"with z outside, f(ζ)/(ζ − z) has a complex slope on a disc holding C, so the loop totals 0, not f(z); the rim technique is for z inside","w":["nothing; the rim fixes f outside as well as inside","the total is −f(z) for z outside, by the direction of the loop","the total is f(z) only when z is on C itself"],"why":"The kernel has its pole at z; outside the loop it catches nothing."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["26h"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["26h"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"26h1.1","q":"What is a pole of order m, what is the residue of f there, and what does the pole-split technique say?","a":"f = g/(z − w)ᵐ near w, with g having a complex slope at w and g(w) ≠ 0; the residue is b₁, the coefficient of 1/(z − w); and f = b_m/(z − w)ᵐ + ⋯ + b₁/(z − w) + h(z), with h having a complex slope at w","w":["a point where f = 0 to order m; the residue is f′(w); and f = (z − w)ᵐ h(z)","f = g/(z − w)ᵐ; the residue is b_m, the coefficient of the highest power; and f splits as b_m/(z − w)ᵐ + h(z) only","a point where f has no value; the residue is g(w) for every m; and f = g(w)/(z − w) + h(z)"],"why":"For order 1, b₁ = g(w), the value of (z − w)f(z) at w."},
 {"u":"26h2.1","q":"What does the residue technique say, and why does each pole contribute only 2πi times its residue?","a":"∮_C f = 2πi times the sum of the residues at the poles inside C; of each trouble part, the powers 1/(z − w)ᵏ with k ≥ 2 have undoings and total 0, the rest of f totals 0 by the disc-undoing technique, and 1/(z − w) alone totals 2πi","w":["∮_C f = 2πi times the number of poles inside; each pole totals 2πi whatever its residue","∮_C f = the sum of the residues; the 2πi cancels round a full loop","∮_C f = 2πi times the sum of the residues at every pole of f, inside C or not"],"why":"A whole loop’s total is fixed by a few coefficients."},
 {"u":"26h3.1","q":"What does the root-count technique say, and why is each root inside counted once?","a":"for a polynomial p with no root on C, (1/2πi) ∮_C p′/p is the number of roots inside, with repeats; p′/p = Σ 1/(z − rⱼ), each term a pole of order 1 with residue 1, and the terms with rⱼ outside total 0","w":["(1/2πi) ∮_C p is the number of roots inside; each root has residue 1 in p","(1/2πi) ∮_C p′/p is the degree of p whatever C is; every root counts","(1/2πi) ∮_C p/p′ counts the roots; each root has residue 2πi"],"why":"A loop counts roots without finding any."},
 {"u":"26h4.1","q":"What is wrong with “Res(e^z/z², 0) = e⁰ = 1, by putting z = 0 in e^z”?","a":"putting in the point is the technique for a pole of order 1; here the order is 2, so the residue is the coefficient g₁ of z in e^z, which is 1 only by luck: for e^(2z)/z² the same move gives 1 and the residue is 2","w":["nothing; the residue is always g(w)","the residue is 0, since 1/z² has an undoing","the residue is e⁰/2, since the order is 2"],"why":"At order m the residue is g_(m − 1), not g₀."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["26i"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["26i"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"26i1.1","q":"What does the part-reading technique say, and how does it give the sum formulas for cos and sin in one line?","a":"find one complex number two ways, each as floor part plus i times upright part, and the parts agree; e^(i(a + b)) = e^(ia) e^(ib), multiplied out, gives cos(a + b) = cos a cos b − sin a sin b and sin(a + b) = sin a cos b + cos a sin b","w":["take the size of both sides; |e^(i(a + b))| = |e^(ia)| + |e^(ib)| gives both formulas","find two complex numbers with one floor part; e^(ia) + e^(ib) gives cos(a + b) = cos a + cos b","flip both sides; the flip of e^(i(a + b)) gives the sum formulas for tan"],"why":"One complex equation is two real ones."},
 {"u":"26i2.1","q":"What does the many-angle technique say, and why does the plane-adding technique prove it in one line?","a":"(cos θ + i sin θ)ⁿ = cos nθ + i sin nθ for every whole n; the left is (e^(iθ))ⁿ = e^(inθ) by the plane-adding technique, so cos nθ and sin nθ are the parts of the two-term power multiplied out","w":["(cos θ + i sin θ)ⁿ = cosⁿθ + i sinⁿθ; powers go inside each part","(cos θ + i sin θ)ⁿ = n cos θ + i n sin θ; the plane-adding technique adds n copies","(cos θ + i sin θ)ⁿ = cos θⁿ + i sin θⁿ; the angle is raised to the power"],"why":"Multiplying turns adds their angles."},
 {"u":"26i3.1","q":"What does the power-to-sum technique say, and why does a power of the cosine come out as a sum of cosines?","a":"cos θ = (e^(iθ) + e^(−iθ))/2 and sin θ = (e^(iθ) − e^(−iθ))/(2i); multiplying out a power gives terms e^(ikθ), which pair up with e^(−ikθ) into cos kθ","w":["cos θ = e^(iθ) and sin θ = e^(−iθ); a power of cos is one cosine of the larger angle","cos θ = (e^(iθ) − e^(−iθ))/2 and sin θ = (e^(iθ) + e^(−iθ))/(2i); the terms pair into sines","cos θ = (e^θ + e^(−θ))/2; a power of cos is a sum of growth functions"],"why":"Sums of cos kθ are the ones rung 16 can integrate at sight."},
 {"u":"26i4.1","q":"What is wrong with “cos³θ = cos 3θ”?","a":"a power of the cosine is a sum of cosines, not one: multiplying out ((e^(iθ) + e^(−iθ))/2)³ gives cos³θ = (cos 3θ + 3 cos θ)/4","w":["it should read cos³θ = 3 cos θ","it should read cos³θ = cos θ³","it holds for every θ by the many-angle technique"],"why":"At θ = 0 both are 1, but at θ = π/3 the left is 1/8 and the right is −1."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["26j"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["26j"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"26j1.1","q":"What does the plane-reach technique say, and why does the sum of 1/(1 + x²) about 0 reach at least 1?","a":"if f, real on the floor, has a complex slope at every z with |z − w| < R, its power sum about w reaches at least R and equals f on that disc; 1/(1 + z²) has a complex slope at every z with |z| < 1, since its only troubles are at ±i","w":["the sum reaches as far as f is smooth on the line; 1/(1 + x²) is smooth, so its sum reaches everywhere","the sum reaches at most R; 1/(1 + z²) fails at ±1","the sum reaches at least R when f has a pole inside the disc; 1/(1 + z²) has one at 0"],"why":"The reach is read off the plane, not the line."},
 {"u":"26j2.1","q":"What does the pole-wall technique say, and what clash does its proof reach?","a":"if the power sum equals f for |z − w| < d and f has a pole p at distance d, the reach is at most d; a longer reach would give the sum a complex slope at p, so it would stay bounded near p, while f grows without ceiling there","w":["the reach is at least d; the clash is that f would be 0 at p","the reach is exactly 2d; the clash is that the sum would have two values at p","the reach is at most d; the clash is that the sum would settle at p to the residue"],"why":"A power sum cannot pass through a pole."},
 {"u":"26j3.1","q":"What does the nearest-trouble technique say, and why do 1/(1 + x²) and 1/(1 − x²) have sums of the same reach about 0?","a":"for f = p/q in lowest terms, the power sum about a real w reaches exactly to the nearest root of q in the plane; the roots ±i and ±1 are all at distance 1 from 0","w":["the sum reaches to the nearest real root of q; both have a real root at 1","the sum reaches to the farthest root of q; both have degree 2","the sum reaches everywhere unless q has a real root; the two differ, 1/(1 + x²) reaching everywhere"],"why":"The plane-reach technique gives at least, the pole wall at most."},
 {"u":"26j4.1","q":"What is wrong with “1/(1 + x²) is smooth on the whole line, so its sum about 0 reaches everywhere”?","a":"the reach is set by the nearest trouble in the plane, and 1/(1 + z²) has poles at ±i, at distance 1; the sum stops at 1 though nothing on the line shows why","w":["1/(1 + x²) is not smooth at x = 1","the sum does reach everywhere; it only settles slowly past 1","the reach is set by where f is largest on the line, which is at 0"],"why":"The line cannot see the poles standing over it."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["26k"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["26k"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"26k1.1","q":"What is the half-disc loop H_R, and what does the half-disc technique say about a total round it?","a":"the floor from −R to R, then the upper half circle of radius R back; the total of f round it is 2πi times the sum of the residues at the poles above the floor of size less than R","w":["the whole circle of radius R; the total is 2πi times the residues at every pole of size less than R","the floor from −R to R and the lower half circle; the total is 2πi times the residues above the floor","the floor from 0 to R and a quarter circle; the total is πi times the residues inside"],"why":"Poles below the floor and beyond the arc count for nothing."},
 {"u":"26k2.1","q":"What does the fading-arc technique say, and how does the loop find ∫ over the line of dx/(1 + x²) without the arctan?","a":"if |f| ≤ M_R on the upper half circle and R·M_R heads to 0, the arc’s integral heads to 0; for 1/(1 + z²) the arc fades, the one pole above is i with residue 1/(2i), and 2πi/(2i) = π","w":["if M_R heads to 0 the arc fades; the poles i and −i give 2πi(1/(2i) − 1/(2i)) = 0","if R·M_R heads to 0 the arc fades; the pole at i has residue 1, giving 2πi","the arc always fades for a quotient of polynomials; the floor piece is then 2π"],"why":"The path fence on the arc is πR·M_R."},
 {"u":"26k3.1","q":"What does the upper-wave technique say, and why e^(ikz) and never cos kz?","a":"for k ≥ 0 and upright part y ≥ 0, |e^(ikz)| = e^(−ky) ≤ 1, so take the floor part of the total of e^(ikz) f(z) round H_R; cos kz is half e^(ikz) and half e^(−ikz), and the second half outgrows every power up the upright, so the arc would not fade","w":["|e^(ikz)| = 1 everywhere; cos kz is avoided only because it has no residue","|e^(ikz)| = e^(ky), which grows; cos kz is avoided because it is 0 on the upright","|e^(ikz)| ≤ 1 below the floor; cos kz is used above the floor and e^(ikz) below"],"why":"e^(iz) shrinks over the top; cos z grows on both sides."},
 {"u":"26k4.1","q":"What does the sine-chord technique say, and what does it do for the sine integral that the fading-arc technique cannot?","a":"sin t ≥ 2t/π for 0 ≤ t ≤ π/2, so ∫ from 0 to π of e^(−R sin t) dt < π/R; for e^(iz)/z the fence gives R·M_R = 1, which does not head to 0, and the chord still makes the arc piece fade, giving ∫ from 0 to ∞ of sin x/x dx = π/2","w":["sin t ≤ t, so the arc piece is at most π; it shows the sine integral diverges","sin t ≥ 2t/π, so the arc piece is exactly π/R; it replaces the residue at 0","sin t ≥ t/2 for all t; it lets comparison with 1/x prove the sine integral settles"],"why":"The integral settles, but not in size; comparison could never prove it."},
 {"u":"26k5.1","q":"What is wrong with “∫ over the line of cos x/(1 + x²) dx = 2πi Res(cos z/(1 + z²), i)”?","a":"cos z grows up the upright, so the arc does not fade and the loop’s total is not the floor piece; use e^(iz)/(1 + z²) and take the floor part, which gives π/e","w":["the residue must be taken at −i as well, giving 0","nothing; cos z stays at most 1 on the arc, as it does on the line","the pole at i lies on the loop, so no residue can be taken"],"why":"The wrong move gives π(e + 1/e)/2, larger than the area under 1/(1 + x²), which is π."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["26l"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["26l"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"26l1.1","q":"What does the two-ends technique say, and what is Γ(s)?","a":"for every real s > 0, ∫ from 0 to ∞ of x^(s − 1) e^(−x) dx converges at both ends: far out the shrinking growth function beats every power, and near 0 the power x^(s − 1) has a finite area since 1 − s < 1; Γ(s) is that area","w":["the integral converges for every real s; Γ(s) is that area","the integral converges far out for s > 0 and near 0 for every s; Γ(s) is its value at s = 1","the integral converges only for whole s; Γ(s) = s!"],"why":"Γ(1) = ∫ e^(−x) dx = 1."},
 {"u":"26l2.1","q":"What does the factorial-step technique say, and which move proves Γ(s + 1) = sΓ(s)?","a":"Γ(s + 1) = s Γ(s) for every real s > 0, so Γ(n + 1) = n! by induction from Γ(1) = 1; integration by parts, x^s against the slope of −e^(−x), with the end pieces heading to 0","w":["Γ(s + 1) = (s + 1)Γ(s), so Γ(n) = n!; a change of variable x = y² proves it","Γ(s + 1) = s Γ(s) for whole s only; counting arrangements proves it","Γ(s + 1) = Γ(s) + s; splitting the area at x = 1 proves it"],"why":"So Γ(5) = 4! = 24."},
 {"u":"26l3.1","q":"What does the bell-area technique say, and how is the area under the bell squeezed?","a":"∫ from 0 to ∞ of e^(−y²) dy = √π/2, so Γ(1/2) = √π; the square of the area is squeezed between two runs built from the areas under powers of sin, both heading to π/4","w":["the area is √π, so Γ(1/2) = 2√π; it is found from an undoing of e^(−y²)","the area is π/2, so Γ(1/2) = π; it is squeezed between two rectangles","the area is √π/2, so Γ(1/2) = √π/2; it is read off the power sum of e^(−y²) term by term"],"why":"Half a factorial is a turn in disguise."},
 {"u":"26l4.1","q":"What does the step-down technique say, and why is continuing a function past its formula not a free choice in the plane?","a":"for real s other than 0, −1, −2, …, Γ(s + n)/(s(s + 1)⋯(s + n − 1)) is the same for every whole n with s + n > 0, and it carries Γ and its step technique below 0; two functions with complex slopes on a disc that agree on a smaller disc have the same pieces uₖ, so they agree on all of it","w":["Γ(s) is set to 0 for s ≤ 0; in the plane any smooth continuation will do","the step technique is run upward to reach every s; a continuation is free so long as it is continuous","Γ(s) for s < 0 is the same area ∫ x^(s − 1) e^(−x) dx; the area leaves no choice"],"why":"A function with a complex slope cannot be changed in one place alone."},
 {"u":"26l7.1","q":"What is wrong with “Γ(n) = n!”?","a":"the count is shifted by one: Γ(n + 1) = n!, so Γ(5) = 4! = 24, not 120","w":["Γ(n) = (n + 1)!, so Γ(5) = 720","Γ(n) = n!/2, so Γ(5) = 60","nothing; Γ(5) = 5! = 120"],"why":"Γ(1) = 0! = 1, and Γ(0) has no value."},
 {"u":"26l5.1","q":"What does the between-factorials technique say Γ(n + θ + 1) is close to? Where do its two fences come from?","a":"n! · n^θ, within the factors 1 − 2/n and 1 + 1/n; from the tangent lines at 1 of u^θ, which bends down, and of u^(θ − 1), which bends up","w":["n! + θ · (n + 1)!, exactly; from the factorial-step technique alone","(n + θ)!, by definition; from the bell-area technique","n! · θ, within 1/n; from the chord between n! and (n + 1)!"],"why":"26l5: x^(n + θ) is fenced above by n^θ xⁿ(1 − θ + θx/n) and below by n^(θ − 1) xⁿ⁺¹(2 − θ − (1 − θ)x/n), and the areas against e^(−x) are factorials."},
 {"u":"26l6.1","q":"How fast does the area under (1 − x²)ⁿ on [−1, 1] head to 0? How does Γ come into the proof?","a":"like √π/√n: √n times the area heads to √π; the area is √π · n!/Γ(n + 3/2), by halving the factors of I₂ₙ₊₁, and Γ(n + 3/2) is close to n!√n","w":["like 1/n: n times the area heads to π; Γ is the area itself","like 2⁻ⁿ; Γ(n) bounds (1 − x²)ⁿ from above","it does not head to 0, since the height stays 1; Γ does not come in"],"why":"26l6: I₂ₙ₊₁ = n! Γ(3/2)/Γ(n + 3/2), and between-factorials at θ = ½ puts Γ(n + 3/2) within the factors 1 − 2/n and 1 + 1/n of n!√n."});
